"""Serialize the individually authored problem index; no automatic verdict inference."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,datetime
P=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
I={x['id']:x for x in json.loads((P/'items.json').read_text())}
C={x['id']:x for x in json.loads((P/'claims.json').read_text())}
R=[]
def p(n,title,items,claims,said,today,why,worth):
 its=[I[f'SA06-{i}'] for i in items]; cs=[C[f'C-SA06-{i}'] for i in claims]
 R.append(dict(id=f'MP-SA06-{n}',title=title,occurrences=[{'item':x['id'],'cote':14,'scans':x['scans'],'lines':x['lines'],'source_entries':x['sources']} for x in its],as_he_states=said,plausible_today=today,state_of_art='As of 2026-11-19. '+' '.join(x['id']+': '+x['fate'] for x in cs),why_open=why,worth=worth,refs=list(dict.fromkeys(k for x in cs for k in x['refs']))))
p(100,'Unfinished algebraicity criterion for a mixed H¹ class',[201],[201], 'Scan89 ends with a request to prove that an unspecified class is algebraic; the condition and quantifiers are unfinished.','The normalized divisor/Hom correspondence is precise and classical; an arithmetic Tate statement requires a specified field and Galois invariance.','No theorem can be matched to an omitted condition; lack of identification is not evidence of novelty.','Worth a targeted ink/context reconstruction, not an attempted proof of an unspecified statement.')
p(101,'Finite freeness of numerical cycle classes',[203],[203], 'The numerical row of scan92 asks whether the group is free of finite type.','For smooth projective varieties with a Weil cohomology, the integral numerical quotient is finite free.','The question has an established answer; the excerpt does not give its proof.','Worth retaining as a precise historical match to Kleiman Theorem3.5, requiring no new research.')
p(102,'Finite differences between cycle equivalences',[203,204],[204,239], 'The chart asks Groupe fini? and marks maps between algebraic, homological, torsion and numerical relations with question marks.','State each equivalence relation and coefficient ring separately; numerical finite generation does not control the Griffiths quotient.','Clemens gives an infinite-dimensional rational obstruction to some proposed finite differences; the chart is not a single true theorem.','Worth reconstructing each row individually and attaching known counterexamples.')
p(103,'Abelian representatives for continuous cycle quotients',[203,246],[204,249], 'The chart asks V.A.? and later sheets use a universal abelian quotient of cycle families.','Universal regular homomorphisms exist in certain codimensions, including codimension2, but do not represent the entire Chow group.','Mumford obstructs full representability; regularity and the cycle-family set-map issue require explicit hypotheses.','Worth a bounded comparison with algebraic representatives, keeping their universal property separate from group isomorphism.')
p(104,'Integral and rational cohomological rows of the chart',[204],[204,272,274], 'Scan92 has queried integral-homology and rational-homology arrows, including topological K-theory notation.','Retain torsion and coefficient distinctions; a Qℓ statement cannot be upgraded to a Zℓ isomorphism or finite error without torsion control.','The crossed arrows and undefined topological notation do not specify a complete comparison theorem.','Worth checking the scan and neighboring notation before matching any integral theorem.')
p(105,'Removing the nonzero-section hypothesis in the ampleness argument',[218,219],[218,219,220], 'Scan95 asks whether the section hypothesis can be suppressed after the two lemmas.','The Euler-growth criterion holds under its proper/projective hypotheses; strict curve positivity alone does not imply ampleness.','A strictly nef line bundle with square0 gives the obstruction to the naive section-free strengthening. The sources do not establish the author’s historical reason for abandoning it.','Worth separating the exact induction hypotheses from the false strictly nef strengthening.')
p(106,'Removing projectivity from the draft ampleness proof',[218],[216,218,219], 'The last line of scan95 asks whether one can suppress X projectif.','Mumford–Oda VIII Theorem1.11 gives an Euler-characteristic criterion for proper schemes over a field; this answers the suitably formulated proper version.','The hyperplane induction on the page uses projectivity; the later criterion needs its own proof and exact properness assumptions.','Worth matching the proper theorem to the manuscript formulations; not an open ampleness problem in that form.')
p(107,'Strict positivity on curves versus ampleness',[219,220],[220,221,222], 'Scan96 asks whether strict positivity on every curve forces the class into the ample region; scan100 explores top intersections.','Strict nefness is weaker than ampleness, even with positive top self-intersection in dimension3.','Mumford’s surface and the checked projective-bundle threefold give concrete counterexamples; closed-cone boundary behavior is the obstruction.','Worth developing only as a counterexample and hypothesis-classification exercise.')
p(108,'Euler-characteristic sign for an isotropic strict-nef divisor',[224],[226], 'Scan96 ends with an expected inequality for χ(D), with the sign itself uncertain.','On a smooth surface with D²=0, χ(nD)=χ(O)−nD·K/2; a sign needs an additional assertion about D·K and the intended n.','The uncertain operator and suppressed numerical hypotheses prevent a universal sign conclusion.','Worth an ink check plus a Riemann–Roch type check, rather than a conjectural sign theorem.')
p(109,'The α-map and polar cycle cones',[223],[225], 'Scan98 asks α_(d−1)(P_(d−1)^{+0})⊂P_1^+ and proposes a generalization with A^(2d−i).','No precise claim is recoverable until α, polar conventions and grading are specified.','The nearby higher-cycle cone counterexamples do not settle an undefined α-map.','Worth locating the preceding definition; no novelty assessment is justified.')
p(110,'Nef divisor powers and top intersection',[222],[223,227], 'Scan100 asks whether D·C≥0 for every curve implies nonnegative higher powers and D^d≥0, and uses a cubic expansion.','For projective varieties, nef divisor powers pair nonnegatively with effective cycles by approximation with ample divisors.','This is established; the polynomial calculation alone was not the missing positivity input.','Worth recording the exact divisor argument and keeping it separate from higher-codimension nefness.')
p(111,'Products of nef higher-cycle classes',[223],[224], 'Scan100 asks for nonnegative pairings/products of the polar cones in complementary codimensions.','There is no universal product-closure theorem for arbitrary nef higher-codimension classes.','DELV2011 gives explicit abelian-variety counterexamples and identifies the 1964 Grothendieck letter as the historical question.','Worth a small exact worked example from DELV, with the cone and coefficient conventions fixed.')
p(112,'Irreducible-pencil control of Picard restriction',[225,226],[228,229], 'Scan103 presumes that irreducible limiting curves in suitable pencils force the indicated kernel behavior.','For a regular pencil with geometrically integral fibres, vertical divisors control generic restriction; a special-fibre injectivity theorem needs additional generality hypotheses.','The draft does not specify the pencil quantifiers or specialization step. Starr’s precise very-general cases are narrower.','Worth formulating and proving the generic-fibre statement first, then auditing specialization separately.')
p(113,'Local finite-generation and finite-error package',[227],[230,234], 'Scans106–107 conjecture local J,N,A finiteness in ranges and cite a Mumford–Hodge surface case.','Codimension1 has local Picard and parafactorial results under normality, excellence, depth and dimension assumptions; no general higher-Chow package is supplied.','The local equivalence convention and N are not fully defined; surface discriminant lattices do not identify an arbitrary local Picard group.','Worth reconstructing the local definitions and testing codimension1 before extending the package.')
p(114,'Local restriction and the critical index',[227,228],[231], 'Scan106 gives local restriction maps with finite-error and characteristic qualifications and singles out a critical case.','Bhatt–de Jong Theorem0.1 gives a precise local Picard injectivity theorem in dimension≥4, with depth in characteristic0 and p-primary kernel in characteristicp.','Higher codimensions and surjectivity are not consequences of that theorem; changing the singularity hypotheses changes the answer.','Worth a theorem-by-theorem hypothesis comparison in codimension1.')
p(115,'Vanishing of local Gysin maps',[228],[232], 'Scan107 asks for vanishing of the local principal-divisor pushforward and treats the open-complement comparison as a consequence.','Localization gives the consequence once vanishing is proved; principality alone only kills the divisor action on pulled-back classes.','The page supplies no proof that all cycles on the divisor extend from the ambient space.','Worth searching for an exact local counterexample or a sufficient extension hypothesis; do not infer it from Picard restriction.')
p(116,'Integral local intersection pairings',[229],[233,234], 'Scan107 asks to define intersection forms with values in Z.','For resolved normal complex surface singularities, exceptional curves have a negative-definite integral matrix; an integral higher-Chow pairing needs a separately defined theory.','The manuscript asks for the construction itself. The cited surface theorem does not provide the missing general objects.','Worth a controlled surface model, including its discriminant group and the exact local cycle quotient.')
p(117,'A cycle-valued vanishing-cycle theory',[229,264,265],[233,235], 'Scan107 asks to define a theory of cycles évanouissants attached to the displayed local maps.','Cohomological vanishing cycles and invariant cycles are established; a cycle-level theory requires specified realization, support and equivalence relation.','No singular correspondence formalism is constructed on these sheets; the cancelled bound is not a replacement.','Worth making the desired functor and duality explicit on one surface singularity.')
p(118,'Natural complement in the local J¹ group',[230],[235], 'Scan108 asks for a natural complementary part of J¹(Y) relative to J¹(X).','For abelian varieties a complement exists up to isogeny after a polarization choice; a canonical integral complement is not automatic.','Semisimplicity up to isogeny does not supply the requested canonicity or integral splitting.','Worth isolating the isogeny-category statement and computing its dependence on polarization.')
p(119,'Meaning of J¹(X)=J¹(Y)^π',[230],[235], 'Scan108 asks to give meaning to the superscript π and the invariant formula.','A cohomological monodromy action can be specified for a smooth proper Lefschetz pencil; a group object and its action on the proposed J must be constructed separately.','The symbol π is not defined and cohomological invariant cycles do not by themselves define algebraic group invariants.','Worth identifying the intended base/pencil and testing whether the action exists algebraically.')
p(120,'Hodge-index interpretation in the local example',[230],[233,234], 'Scan108 asks to interpret negativity of N¹(X) as a Hodge-index statement.','Use the negative-definite exceptional intersection lattice only under the normal surface resolution hypotheses and with the correct N¹ identification.','A finite discriminant group, a numerical divisor space and a local Picard group are distinct.','Worth writing the exact lattice-to-local-class-group comparison in a single normal surface example.')
p(121,'Natural complement in N¹(Y)',[230],[235], 'Scan109 repeats the complement problem for the N¹ quotient.','An orthogonal complement in a finite-dimensional nondegenerate rational pairing is available after hypotheses are proved; it is not a canonical integral direct summand.','The needed pairing, nondegeneracy and integral saturation are not established on the page.','Worth separating rational orthogonal decomposition from integral discriminant data.')
p(122,'Meaning of N¹(X)=N¹(Y)^π',[230],[235], 'Scan109 asks to give meaning to a second invariant formula, now for N¹.','Only a specified monodromy representation and identified algebraic-cycle subspace make this a definite assertion.','A cohomological invariant-cycle theorem need not preserve or generate the chosen algebraic quotient.','Worth comparing the stated N quotient with the cohomological invariant subspace without assuming equality.')
p(123,'Odd- and even-dimensional local vanishings',[230],[230,235], 'The lower part of scan109 marks N=2m+1 as a case to prove and brackets an even-dimensional analogue.','No universal statement follows until N, the local cycle theory, dimensions and finite-error qualification are fixed.','The source itself leaves the claim unproved; the searched local Picard results settle only a restricted codimension1 part.','Worth a dimension-by-dimension audit after recovering the notation.')
p(124,'Global weak Lefschetz for rational-equivalence groups',[232],[237], 'Scan111 proposes restriction isomorphisms/injections in explicit codimension ranges, with exc.p? qualifications.','The rational Chow weak Lefschetz conjecture has the stated stable range i<dimY/2; Picard cases have classical theorems and infinitesimal K-cohomology has partial results.','Patel–Ravindra explicitly distinguish infinitesimal lifting from algebraization; finite integral errors are a stronger issue.','Worth one focused run comparing a fixed codimension and class of varieties with the modern conjecture.')
p(125,'Global Gysin surjectivity on Chow groups',[232],[238], 'Scan111 gives a separate pushforward range from a smooth hyperplane Y to X.','The unrestricted Chow surjectivity is false already for CH0 of a K3 surface; quotients modulo algebraic equivalence have a different behavior.','Mumford’s theorem prevents a fixed curve from parameterizing all zero-cycles on a K3.','Worth retaining the sharp K3 counterexample and reformulating only in a specified quotient.')
p(126,'Hard Lefschetz modulo algebraic equivalence',[233],[239], 'Scan112 asks for bijectivity modulo finite groups of the Lefschetz map on A_a.','On general smooth projective varieties this is false even rationally; on cohomological algebraic classes it becomes the distinct standard Lefschetz conjecture.','A very general quintic threefold has an infinite-dimensional Griffiths quotient, obstructing surjectivity from finite-rank divisor classes.','Worth documenting the exact failure and avoiding substitution of numerical equivalence for the written a.')
p(127,'Lefschetz maps on algebraically trivial or τ subgroups',[233,234],[240], 'Scan112 revises the subgroup/quotient formulas and finite-error qualifications.','The unrestricted shifted surjectivity is false for a K3: Pic⁰ and Pic^τ vanish while CH0⁰ does not.','The proposed subgroup range does not track the extra Chow filtration needed by refined conjectures.','Worth testing precise filtered replacements only after fixing the meaning of τ.')
p(128,'General powers on the whole Chow group',[234],[241], 'The last implication on scan112 proposes whole-Chow injectivity/surjectivity ranges.','No such universal surjectivity holds; refined Abel–Jacobi-trivial injectivity and Beauville-graded results are different statements.','The K3 divisor-product image is one-dimensional while CH0 is not finite-dimensional.','Worth using this as a diagnostic example before attempting any general Lefschetz package.')
p(129,'Affine-complement rational Chow vanishing',[235,238],[242,243,245], 'Scans113 and116 mark vanishings of A_i^r(U) with characteristic exceptions and double question marks.','Localization identifies the needed Gysin image; no general vanishing follows from affine cohomological dimension.','The cycle pushforward surjectivity can fail even when the cohomological analogue holds.','Worth analyzing a specified U and exact degree, with localization written in dimension grading.')
p(130,'Affine-complement algebraic-equivalence vanishing',[235,238],[239,242,245], 'The second row on scan113 and upper row on116 use a rather than r, with a separate question.','This quotient must be checked independently; rational equivalence, algebraic equivalence and numerical equivalence cannot be interchanged.','The Griffiths obstruction survives algebraic equivalence; degree-specific claims need more hypotheses.','Worth a single explicit threefold/fourfold test with the intended quotient fixed.')
p(131,'Primitive classes and the punctured cone',[235,236],[243], 'Scan113 expects cone/primitive identifications and writes dimension2n+1 and a kernel exponent n−2i.','The cone has dimensionn+1; its Chow group is a Lefschetz cokernel, and primitive cohomology uses exponentn−2i+1.','The dimension and exponent readings are incompatible with standard grading; primitive Chow decomposition is not given.','Worth an ink check and a complete line-bundle localization derivation.')
p(132,'Cancelled cohomological-dimension theorem',[212],[255], 'Scan114 presents Th.4 and a reduction, then cancels the whole paragraph.','A precise valid bound needs a specified residue-field and coefficient hypothesis; the visible literal version is false over R[[t]].','The statement was withdrawn on the page; no documented reason for cancellation is known.','Worth identifying its exact SGA2/SGA4 counterpart only after recovering the omitted hypotheses.')
p(133,'Conditional threefold and surface tables',[237],[244], 'Scans115–116 repeatedly mark OK only if rational equivalence equals Albanese equivalence.','The conditional implications remain conditional; torsion Albanese isomorphism is established, full injectivity generally false.','Mumford’s Albanese-kernel obstruction invalidates the universal premise.','Worth retaining the table as a conditional diagram and labeling every use of its premise.')
p(134,'Fourfold critical restriction',[238],[245,237], 'Scan116 questions A_r²(X)^τ→A_r²(Y)^τ at the critical degree.','No universal integral answer follows from the checked rational motivic theorems; formulate a specified Abel–Jacobi or Bloch–Beilinson piece.','The critical degree lies beyond the elementary weak Lefschetz range and τ is not fully identified.','Worth an exact comparison with a modern filtered Chow conjecture for one class of fourfolds.')
p(135,'Fourfold rational-equivalence pushforward',[238],[245,238], 'Scan116 asks whether A_1^r(Y)→A_1^r(X) is surjective.','Treat this as a separate one-cycle generation question; cohomological hard Lefschetz does not answer it.','The checked abelian and conditional Chow results have narrower hypotheses and gradings.','Worth choosing an explicit fourfold family and testing the cycle generation map.')
p(136,'Fourfold τ-subgroup pushforward',[238],[245,240], 'The next scan116 row asks for surjectivity on the τ subgroups.','A meaningful modern version needs an explicit τ relation and filtration, with integral errors controlled separately.','The page’s double arrows do not supply those structures or a proof.','Worth resolving the notation before further literature matching.')
p(137,'Fourfold algebraic-equivalence pushforward',[238],[245,239], 'Scan116 asks for A_1^a(Y)→A_1^a(X) bijectivity and calls it numérique.','The written algebraic-equivalence quotient is larger than the numerical quotient; its Griffiths part must be retained.','The label numérique does not authorize replacing a by numerical equivalence.','Worth comparing both quotients in the same example and locating the intended usage of the label.')
p(138,'Fourfold E² divisor-to-one-cycle comparison',[238],[245,239], 'Scan116 marks E²:A_a¹(X)→A_a³(X) with a question.','On cohomological algebraic classes this is a standard-conjecture comparison; modulo algebraic equivalence extra Griffiths data remain.','A rational Chow-motive theorem for abelian varieties is not the general integral assertion.','Worth first restricting to abelian fourfolds and keeping the Beauville weight visible.')
p(139,'Fourfold τ-Lefschetz comparison',[238],[245,246], 'The last table row on116 questions A_r²(X)^τ→A_r³(X)^τ and draws implications.','The strongest plausible replacement uses a specified motivic grading and shifted degree, not the whole τ group.','The sheet gives neither the filtration nor the algebraicity of projectors needed for that replacement.','Worth a type-correct modern reformulation; do not call the drawn implications proofs.')
p(140,'Fourfold lower affine vanishings',[238],[245,242], 'The bottom row on116 separately asks A_0^r(U)=A_1^r(U)=0.','Localization makes these exact generation questions for Chow groups in two different dimensions.','The lower row has r where the upper row has a; merging them would erase a substantive distinction.','Worth keeping two exact localization sequences and seeking concrete examples.')
p(141,'A primitive part of the whole Chow ring',[239],[246,243], 'Scan117 doubts that the whole A_r admits a reasonable primitive-part definition.','Rational Chow motives of abelian schemes have Lefschetz decompositions; induced Chow statements need the extra Beauville grading and shifted weights.','The ordinary Chow ring has kernels and images not controlled by cohomological Lefschetz alone.','Worth a limited abelian-variety comparison, with no universal decomposition asserted.')
p(142,'Bounded parameterization of zero-cycles',[241],[247], 'Scan118 assumes finitely many algebraic families suffice for numerical-trivial zero-cycle classes modulo rational equivalence.','This finite-dimensionality fails on complex surfaces with a nonzero holomorphic2form.','Mumford1969 supplies the obstruction; the later1968letter explicitly retracts the related rectangle expectation.','Worth identifying exactly which narrower varieties have representable CH0.')
p(143,'Rectangle equivalence and the Albanese inverse',[242,244,245,246],[248,249], 'Scans118,126,128 relate rectangle cycles, additivity of δ, and an Albanese isomorphism; a diagonal implication remains queried.','The rectangle is automatically algebraically trivial, but its rational triviality is false in general; conditional equivalences must use the same relation throughout.','The ∼a/∼r mismatch is substantive. Universal regular quotients need not recover every rational-equivalence class.','Worth a complete typed conditional diagram and an elliptic-product counterexample.')
p(144,'Bounded cycles versus bounded K-classes',[248,249],[251,252], 'Scans120–121 propose boundedness criteria; équivalentes is deleted on121 and scan123 retains only a chain of implications.','The common-presentation criterion for bounded vector bundles is established; the cycle comparison needs the definition of K-equivalence and the integral factorial/sign.','Rational Chern-character isomorphism does not settle an undefined integral cycle equivalence.','Worth locating the K-equivalence definition and checking each arrow separately.')
p(145,'The two transporter question marks',[213],[256,257], 'Two unattached question marks stand beside the cancelled start of Lemma1.1 on122.','SGA3 XIII Lemma1.1 gives the exact dimension, unramifiedness and smoothness conditions in the complete setup.','The marks do not specify which implication the author doubted; assigning a false tangent formula to them would be conjectural.','Worth comparing the scan geometry with the printed lemma; no new theorem need be invented.')
p(146,'Cartan proof and restored unirationality argument',[214],[258,259], 'Scan127 instructs that an accidentally cancelled proof be retained, while129 completes the regular-Cartan argument.','SGA3 XIII Theorem3.1, Corollaries3.2–3.3 and Lemma3.4 give the precise published result and Chevalley torus norm argument.','Cancellation here is explicitly an editorial error, not a mathematical retraction.','Worth preserving that documentary distinction and the exact publication match.')
p(147,'Transpose signs for divisor and curve correspondences',[288,298],[202,262,282], 'Scan62 twice warns about signs in transpose/duality formulas and proposes an AQT verification.','The signs can be computed after fixing Poincaré normalization, factor order, trace and covariant/contravariant conventions.','AQT is not expanded and the original sign conventions are incomplete in the packet.','Worth one convention-explicit computation, while leaving the ink and abbreviation unresolved.')
p(148,'Alternative Picard-scheme proof',[288],[262], 'Scan62 proposes a new proof via a homomorphism of Picard group schemes and says the result should move to section1.','Use the reduced Picard identity component for an abelian-variety statement; nonreduced Picard schemes need a group-scheme formulation.','A map on rational points does not specify a scheme morphism or settle infinitesimal structure.','Worth reconstructing the functorial Picard-scheme map under fixed smooth/proper hypotheses.')
p(149,'Integral inverse to Albanese H¹',[292],[270], 'Question1 on71 asks for an integral inverse cycle and gives a rational generic symmetric-power point as sufficient.','Rational inverse cycles always exist; an integral inverse is equivalent to a universal zero-cycle and is not universal, even for surfaces with representable CH0.','Voisin2025 and Alexandrou2026 supply exact counterexamples; representability alone does not give an integral cycle inverse.','Worth a focused study of the additional integral obstruction and positive geometric classes.')
p(150,'Integral inverse to Picard/top-cohomology',[292],[271], 'Question2 on71 asks for an integral inverse from H¹(Pic⁰X) to the top odd cohomology of X, with a proposed abelian-variety reduction conditional on Question1.','The universal assertion fails already for very general complex principally polarized abelian varieties of dimension≥4.','An inverse would algebraize the integral mixed Poincaré divided-power class and imply integral Hodge for one-cycles, contradicted by EGFS.','Worth writing the exact Poincaré obstruction and investigating the smallest positive classes; the general question is settled negatively.')
p(151,'Albanese and the abelianized fundamental group',[295],[272,273,274,275], 'Scan74 queries always surjective/injective endpoints in comparisons among Alb, Pic, cohomology and π1^ab.','Prime-to-characteristic comparison follows from Kummer and duality with a finite NS-torsion term; a full p-primary profinite claim needs more.','Overwritten ?/! and à torsion près cannot be replaced by an unqualified isomorphism.','Worth a finite-coefficient exact-sequence audit with characteristic and NS torsion fixed.')
p(152,'Axiomatizing the dual abelian variety by divisors',[297],[201,202,276], 'The box on76 proposes defining A* axiomatically by the mixed-divisor/Hom formula.','The normalized Poincaré representability property characterizes the dual abelian variety in the usual Picard formalism.','The page states an idea, not a complete axiom system; alternation versus symmetric dual homomorphism must be distinguished.','Worth a short universal-property comparison, not a new category construction.')
p(153,'Top-degree trace sign',[298],[202,277,282], 'Scan78 explicitly requests a sign check beside top-degree identifications and Tate twists.','The trace pairing and exterior-algebra identifications are precise after orientation and Tate conventions; the computed sign is convention-dependent.','No scan is available to decide which written sign was intended.','Worth the saved exterior calculation and subsequent ink review.')
p(154,'Dimension qualification in pushforward transposition',[299],[278,281], 'Scan80 explicitly recalls the equal-dimension hypothesis, while source/target labels change.','For dimensions n,n′ the pushforward shift is2(n′−n) with Tate twistn′−n, and complementary exterior degree uses the correct source dimension.','Suppressing the dimension change produces a type error; the handwritten header cannot silently remain unchanged.','Worth a full degree-and-twist audit of the diagram.')
p(155,'Recovering an integral image from its top wedge',[300],[279,280], 'Scans83/85 ask whether determinant/top-wedge information determines the image and its degree.','The decomposable wedge determines the rational image subspace; determinant content does not determine the integral sublattice.','Two explicit diagonal lattices have the same determinant line and different images.','Worth distinguishing rational span, lattice index and lattice position in the intended claim.')

(P/'problems.json').write_text(json.dumps(R,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
with (P/'progress.log').open('a') as f:f.write(datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat()+f'\tPROBLEMS_SAVED\t{len(R)} individually authored problem records\n')
print({'problems':len(R)})
